#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 840.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199252} :activity/id #uuid "394fea4c-49c6-55ac-b019-2ee697e8297a" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049032 :topic/id #uuid "d4ff589f-1af0-5477-b78d-91f6dfb7f322" :topic/math-academy-id 4134} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199252 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199452 :tutorial/content "The plane region $D$ shown below is defined as\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: 1 \\leq xe^{y}\\leq 4, \\: \\: 1 \\leq x^2e^{-2y} \\leq 3 \\big\\}.\n$$\n\n![](images/17/17e133a50f4a587a26fbf0914c1dc08106ecb822e1e88ba5e7280a10215221c0.png)\n\n \n\nThis region is pretty complicated, which could make it difficult to work with.\n\nSo, we'd like to define a transformation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf T^{-1}: (x,y)\\to (u(x,y),v(x,y))\n$$\n\nthat maps the region $D$ to a rectangular region $\\Delta$ in the $uv$-plane.  \n\n![](images/d2/d26df05b3200a01f2b6fba5a1c04e8d178c88f40e078309c71329bcdfcc91ac2.png)\n\n \n\nHow can we find such a transformation?\n\nLooking at the bounding curves that make up the structure of $D,$ we consider the transformation \n\n$$\n{\\color{red}u}={\\color{red}{xe^{y}}}, \\qquad {\\color{blue}v}={\\color{blue}{x^2e^{-2y}}}.\n$$\n\nNext, we find our expression for $\\Delta$ by writing $D$ in terms of $u$ and $v,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ={T}^{-1}(D) & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le x{e}^{y}\\le 4,\\,\\,1\\le {x}^{2}{e}^{-2y}\\le 3\\} \\\\  & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 4,\\,\\,1\\le v\\le 3\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd that's all there is to it! We've successfully found a transformation $\\mathbf T^{-1}$ that maps the complicated region $D$ in the $xy$-plane to the rectangle $[1,4]\\times[1,3]$ in the $uv$-plane.\n\n![](images/64/6423e8d59e07acda3c8d84e47852ad95f37d9fdf39c3669a8227c224a0cbb54a.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "1f2467b8-6aa5-598d-bd70-646d2f6c5588" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4410 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "81de6675-7ea1-553a-9697-f6798481631b" :step/math-academy-id 27105 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199253}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199253 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198362 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198236 :question/id #uuid "6d2cd5bf-899f-5b81-9512-ee98489d17ac" :question/math-academy-id "e-7322"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a6d98056-016e-520b-b752-d6a529f2c02d" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049373}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198949} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199005} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371443} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371450} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491121} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491131} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491167} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491193} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491196}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Rectangular Preimage Given a Transformation"} :step/id #uuid "a16a89f0-4b6c-571e-8667-09aa727ed945" :step/math-academy-id 27106 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199276}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199254 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198363 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198237 :question/id #uuid "c78f087b-f0e5-58e6-a7a1-92a78c85c4d9" :question/math-academy-id "e-7323"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9156e571-2fe9-5347-95da-c1c97de1a3c9" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050822} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050984}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199004} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199006} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371457} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371464} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371471} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373298} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373374} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491144} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491164} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491173}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Transformation That Maps a Region Onto a Type I Region"} :step/id #uuid "548a6670-5a09-5138-83d9-a4ddc9f4a9dc" :step/math-academy-id 27107} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199255 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199453 :tutorial/content "We often use transformations to map a region in the $xy$-plane to a type I region in the $uv$-plane.\n\nFor example, let $D$ be the region bounded by the curve $(x+y)^2 - y = 2$ and the line $y=1,$  as shown in the diagram below. \n\n![](images/d7/d7df2aa53d64cbd13c784a6dc5d827027f56cc8e63917bbd9a324f7576f3dfa6.png)\n\n \n\nThis is not a type I region because the lower curve is not an explicit function of $x.$ \n\nLet's map $D$ to a type I region in the $uv$-plane. First, let $\\mathbf T$ be the transformation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:(u,v) \\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)).\n$$\n\nThe equations of the bounding curves suggest the following for the transformation $\\mathbf T^{-1}{:}$\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{u}}={\\color{blue}{x+y}}, \\qquad {\\color{red}{v}}={\\color{red}{y}}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\color{blue}{u}}={\\color{blue}{x+y}}$ and ${\\color{red}{v}}={\\color{red}{y}}$ into the equation of the lower curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+y)^{2}-y=2\\,⟹\\,{u}^{2}-v=2\\,⟹\\,v={u}^{2}-2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is the equation of an upward-opening parabola. \n\nSubstituting $y = v$ into the equation of the line, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=1\\,⟹\\,v=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the intersections of the parabola and line in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}v={u}^{2}-2 \\\\ v=1\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}u=\\pm \\sqrt{3} \\\\ v=1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection points are $(\\pm \\sqrt3, 1).$\n\n![](images/5b/5b52258abe08675938210de54c53dafe474e753249341c36833b8286259144ee.png)\n\nFinally, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the following type I representation: \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}^{-1}(D)  = \\big\\{ (u,v) \\: : \\: -\\sqrt3 \\leq u \\leq \\sqrt3, \\quad u^2-2 \\leq v \\leq 1 \\big\\}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "6de07339-5bd1-5441-867a-164b51b62c5e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4411 :tutorial/title "Transformations That Map a Region to a Type I Region"} :step/id #uuid "708c40ea-21c0-5791-80ec-1f7934311a44" :step/math-academy-id 27113 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199254}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199276 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198376 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198018 :question/id #uuid "87c2cf96-8772-5b28-b6d8-a5616b38543f" :question/math-academy-id "e-12337"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "de3fa2fa-304c-583a-8c73-a5ff1db972e2" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198556} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198582} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371920} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371927} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371934} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398500} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491130} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491169} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491212}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Transformation That Maps a Rectangular Region Onto a Given One"} :step/id #uuid "b3d2c730-410c-5244-b2a9-29f37c6ec44b" :step/math-academy-id 44206 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199255}}] :activity/title "Transformations of Regions Between Curves" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186198480}]}
